{
  "rehearsal": "ridgeline volume migration, crash fallback and evidence handling",
  "state": "complete",
  "operational_notes": {
    "data_to_move_before_restoring_fixed_address": "Only the post-image delta crosses back to the old directory: samples whose database sequence is greater than the migration image sequence (in this rehearsal the two catch-up batches), grouped by sequence and replayed in order through the normal ingest API so each batch stays atomic and last-write-wins ordering is preserved. The production history never leaves the old volume; copying the whole candidate file back would move the entire database again for zero logical gain. The record carries full_image_bytes next to per-batch replay bytes so the ratio is explicit: in production the image holds the large history while the two batches are tiny by comparison.",
    "sufficient_evidence_to_resume_collection": [
      "Client-held receipt of batch one (sequence, accepted count, watermark): a confirmed write that must survive any fallback.",
      "Quarantined candidate file (sha256 recorded at crash time, copy verified byte-identical) whose isolated inspection shows the namespace last_sequence advanced past batch one AND every batch-two point stored under one sequence: atomic-commit proof, so batch two is preserved through the delta replay and must not be retransmitted.",
      "Inspection showing last_sequence equal to the batch-one receipt sequence and zero samples above it: atomic-absence proof, so the client retransmits batch two after the fixed address is restored.",
      "Migration image headers (X-Ridge-Sequence, X-Ridge-SHA256) tying candidate lineage to the old directory, plus matching namespace floor/watermark and rollup source_sequence on both sides."
    ],
    "insufficient_evidence_preserve_scene": "If the candidate file cannot be trusted (checksum mismatch while copying, bbolt open or decode errors, torn metadata, or inspection cannot prove batch atomicity): stop writes on both volumes. Leave the crashed file immutable: record sha256/size/mtime, remove write permission, keep a byte copy in quarantine and never open the original with a writer. Keep the old directory untouched. Restore the fixed address on the OLD directory in read-only mode so reads continue while producers keep buffering. Reconcile producer logs against the quarantined file manually. Unreceipted batches are retransmitted only after reconciliation: ingestion is atomic per request and last-write-wins makes identical values idempotent, so a retransmitted batch converges whether or not the original attempt committed. Do not delete the quarantined file until the fixed-address service has acknowledged the reconciled state."
  },
